<sheets><sheet><plain><text x="20" y="512" size="3.4" layer="97" ratio="8">xPort Pro V.92 Fax Card — HP EIO slot form factor: telnet BBS on the xPort Pro RJ45, Group 3 fax on the RJ11</text>
<text x="20" y="504" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">POWER (row A/B): J1 12 V → FB1 → U1 (LM2576S-3.3) → L1 → 3V3. The same 12 V feeds J2 VIN, which UM1724 specifies as 7-12 V, so the Nucleo makes its own MCU rail.</text>
<text x="20" y="499" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">L1 is the block default 100 uH. The LM2576 nomogram wants more inductance at 12 V in than at 5 V in — re-read the L-vs-VIN chart and refit before building.</text>
<text x="20" y="494" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">The header 3V3 pin on J2 is left open on purpose: back-powering it while VIN is live would fight the Nucleo onboard regulator.</text>
<text x="20" y="372" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">USART2 to the xPort Pro: D1 = PA2 = TX → X1 DIN, D0 = PA3 = RX ← X1 DOUT. USART1 to the modem: D8 = PA9 = TX → U3 RXD, D2 = PA10 = RX ← U3 TXD.</text>
<text x="20" y="367" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">TRAP: PA2/PA3 are also the ST-LINK virtual COM port (MB1136 SB62/SB63 ON, SB13/SB14 OFF). Leave it wired for a free USB console, or open SB62/SB63 so the xPort Pro owns the port.</text>
<text x="20" y="362" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">Both links are 3.3 V TTL. The xPort Pro serial channel and the modem module are 3.3 V parts, so there is no level shifter; the 100 R resistors are hot-plug and fault limiting, not translation.</text>
<text x="20" y="224" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">U3 is a controller-based V.92/56k module: 3.3 V, 5 V tolerant serial TTL, DAA and 3 kV isolation on the module, V.92 data to 56 kbps, Group 3 fax to 14.4 kbps Class 1 (TIA/EIA-578; Class 1.0 is T.31).</text>
<text x="20" y="219" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">No hardware flow control. 115200 8N1 against a 14.4 kbps fax channel leaves 8x headroom for an interrupt-driven RX; add RTS/CTS if the firmware ever polls.</text>
<text x="20" y="214" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">TIP and RING run only between U3 and J3. The isolation is inside the module; a test point on that pair would defeat it.</text>
<text x="20" y="96" size="2.2" layer="97" ratio="8">EIO (J5) is a mechanical keep-out. EIO uses conventional-PCI 3.3 V signalling, an STM32 cannot enumerate as a PCI target, and HP does not publish the pinout. This revision takes power from J1, not from the slot.</text>
<frame x1="0" y1="0" x2="520" y2="520" columns="8" rows="6" layer="91" border-left="yes" border-top="yes" border-right="yes" border-bottom="yes"/>
<text x="330" y="8" size="2.2" layer="97">xPort Pro V.92 Fax Card rev A · sheet 1/1 · generated from tools/xport-v92-faxcard-model.mjs · see docs/xport-nucleo-v92-eio-fax-card.md</text></plain>
